Large-scale AI factories for training and inference are increasingly constrained by memory capacity, bandwidth, and tail latency. Yet most AI system simulators continue to focus on computation and communication while treating memory as an implicit or uniform resource. This modeling gap leads to inaccurate performance projections as modern AI workloads span deep memory hierarchies, rely on memory pooling, and exhibit highly dynamic access patterns. This position paper argues that memory tiering and memory usage must be treated as first-class, co-equal resources in AI-factory simulators. Realistic simulation requires explicit modeling of memory allocation, placement, movement, and contention. Using the open Chakra execution trace format as a concrete example, we show how memory operations can be represented as explicit trace entities alongside compute and communication. We discuss implications for large-scale simulators such as ASTRA-sim when memory behavior is not directly encoded in traces, and we use LMCache as a public example to illustrate how KV-cache placement and tier transitions fundamentally shape system-level performance. We conclude by outlining telemetry requirements and the need to model emerging multi-tier and pooled memory architectures.